\id RUT - Berean Standard Bible
\h Ruth
\toc2 Ruth
\toc1 Ruth
\mt1 Ruth
\c 1
\s1 Naomi Becomes a Widow
\r (1 Timothy 5:3–16)
\m
\v 1 In the days when the judges ruled, there was a famine in the land. And \zwd a certain man|Q363660\zwd* from Bethlehem in Judah, with \zwd his wife|Q2596433\zwd* and \zwd two sons|Q9026972,Q30246326\zwd*, went to reside in the land of Moab.
\v 2 \zwd The man|Q363660\zwd*’s name was \zwd Elimelech|Q363660\zwd*, \zwd his wife’s|Q2596433\zwd* name was \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd*, and the names of \zwd his two sons|Q9026972,Q30246326\zwd* were \zwd Mahlon|Q9026972\zwd* and \zwd Chilion|Q30246326\zwd*. They were Ephrathites from Bethlehem in Judah, and they entered the land of Moab and settled there.
\b
\m
\v 3 Then \zwd \zwd Naomi’s|Q2596433\zwd* husband Elimelech|Q363660\zwd* died, and she was left with \zwd her two sons|Q9026972,Q30246326\zwd*,
\v 4 who took Moabite women as \zwd their wives|Q1697019,Q1774982\zwd*, one named \zwd Orpah|Q1697019\zwd* and the other named \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd*.
\b
\m And after they had lived in Moab about ten years,
\v 5 both \zwd Mahlon|Q9026972\zwd* and \zwd Chilion|Q30246326\zwd* also died, and \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* was left without \zwd her two sons|id="Q9026972,Q30246326" t="2"\zwd* and without \zwd her husband|Q363660\zwd*.
\s1 Ruth’s Loyalty to Naomi
\b
\m
\v 6 When \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* heard in Moab that the LORD had attended to His people by providing them with food, she and \zwd her daughters-in-law|Q1697019,Q1774982\zwd* prepared to leave the land of Moab.
\v 7 Accompanied by \zwd her two daughters-in-law|Q1697019,Q1774982\zwd*, she left the place where she had been living and set out on the road leading back to the land of Judah.
\b
\m
\v 8 Then \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* said to \zwd her two daughters-in-law|Q1697019,Q1774982\zwd*, “Go back, each of you to your mother’s home. May the LORD show you loving devotion,\f + \fr 1:8 \ft Forms of the Hebrew chesed are translated here and in most cases throughout the Scriptures as loving devotion; the range of meaning includes love, goodness, kindness, faithfulness, and mercy, as well as loyalty to a covenant.\f* as you have shown to your dead and to me.
\v 9 May the LORD enable each of you to find rest in the home of your new husband.”
\b
\m And she kissed them as they wept aloud
\v 10 and said, “Surely we will return with you to your people.”
\b
\m
\v 11 But \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* replied, “Return home, \zwd my daughters|Q1697019,Q1774982\zwd*. Why would you go with me? Are there still sons in my womb to become your husbands?
\v 12 Return home, \zwd my daughters|Q1697019,Q1774982\zwd*. Go on, for I am too old to have another husband. Even if I thought there was hope for me to have a husband tonight and to bear sons,
\v 13 would you wait for them to grow up? Would you refrain from having husbands? No, \zwd my daughters|Q1697019,Q1774982\zwd*, it is much more bitter for me than for you, because the hand of the LORD has gone out against me.”
\b
\m
\v 14 Again they wept aloud, and \zwd Orpah|Q1697019\zwd* kissed \zwd her mother-in-law|Q2596433\zwd* goodbye, but \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd* clung to her.
\b
\m
\v 15 “Look,” said \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd*, “\zwd your sister-in-law|Q1697019\zwd* has gone back to her people and her gods; follow her back home.”
\b
\m
\v 16 But \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd* replied:
\b
\q1 “Do not urge me to leave you
\q2 or to turn from following you.
\q1 For wherever you go, I will go,
\q2 and wherever you live, I will live;
\q1 your people will be my people,
\q2 and your God will be my God.
\q1
\v 17 Where you die, I will die,
\q2 and there I will be buried.
\q1 May the LORD punish me,
\q2 and ever so severely,
\q1 if anything but death
\q2 separates you and me.”
\b
\m
\v 18 When \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* saw that \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd* was determined to go with her, she stopped trying to persuade her.
\s1 The Return to Bethlehem
\b
\m
\v 19 So \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* and \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd* traveled until they came to Bethlehem. When they entered Bethlehem, the whole town was stirred because of them, and the women of the town exclaimed, “Can this be \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd*?”
\b
\m
\v 20 “Do not call me \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd*,\f + \fr 1:20 \ft Naomi means pleasant.\f*” she replied. “Call me \zwd Mara|Q2596433\zwd*,\f + \fr 1:20 \ft Mara means bitter.\f* because the Almighty \f + \fr 1:20 \ft Hebrew Shaddai; also in verse 21\f* has dealt quite bitterly with me.
\v 21 I went away full, but the LORD has brought me back empty. Why call me \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd*? After all, the LORD has testified against me, and the Almighty has afflicted me.”
\b
\m
\v 22 So \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* returned from the land of Moab with \zwd her daughter-in-law Ruth the Moabitess|Q1774982\zwd*. And they arrived in Bethlehem at the beginning of the barley harvest.
\c 2
\s1 Boaz Meets Ruth
\m 
\v 1 Now \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* had a relative on \zwd her husband’s|id="Q363660" t="2"\zwd* side, a prominent man of noble character from the clan of \zwd Elimelech|id="Q363660" t="2"\zwd*, whose name was \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 And \zwd Ruth the Moabitess|Q1774982\zwd* said to \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd*, “Please let me go into the fields and glean heads of grain after someone in whose sight I may find favor.” 
\b
\m “Go ahead, \zwd my daughter|Q1774982\zwd*,” \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 So \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd* departed and went out into the field and gleaned after the harvesters. And she happened to come to the part of the field belonging to \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd*, who was from the clan of \zwd Elimelech|Q363660\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Just then \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* arrived from Bethlehem and said to the harvesters, “The LORD be with you.” 
\b
\m “The LORD bless you,” they replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 And \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* asked \zwd the foreman of his harvesters|\zwd*, “Whose young woman is this?” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zwd The foreman|\zwd* answered, “She is \zwd the Moabitess|Q1774982\zwd* who returned with \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* from the land of Moab. 
\v 7 She has said, ‘Please let me glean and gather among the sheaves after the harvesters.’ So she came out and has continued from morning until now, except that she rested a short time in the shelter.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* said to \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd*, “Listen, \zwd my daughter|Q1774982\zwd*. Do not go and glean in another field, and do not go away from this place, but stay here close to my servant girls. 
\v 9 Let your eyes be on the field they are harvesting, and follow along after these girls. Indeed, I have ordered the young men not to touch you. And when you are thirsty, go and drink from the jars the young men have filled.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 At this, she fell on her face, bowing low to the ground, and said to him, “Why have I found such favor in your eyes that you should take notice of me, even though I am a foreigner?” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* replied, “I have been made fully aware of all you have done for \zwd your mother-in-law|Q2596433\zwd* since the death of \zwd your husband|Q9026972\zwd*, how you left \zwd your father|\zwd* and \zwd mother|\zwd* and the land of your birth, and how you came to a people you did not know before. 
\v 12 May the LORD repay your work, and may you receive a rich reward from the LORD, the God of Israel, under whose wings you have taken refuge.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 “\zwd My lord|Q212253\zwd*,” she said, “may I continue to find favor in your eyes, for you have comforted and spoken kindly to \zwd your maidservant|Q1774982\zwd*, though I am not like one of your servant girls.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 At mealtime \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* said to her, “Come over here; have some bread and dip it into the vinegar sauce.” So she sat down beside the harvesters, and he offered her roasted grain, and she ate and was satisfied and had some left over. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 When \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd* got up to glean, \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* ordered his young men, “Even if she gathers among the sheaves, do not insult her. 
\v 16 Rather, pull out for her some stalks from the bundles and leave them for her to gather. Do not rebuke her.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 So \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd* gathered grain in the field until evening. And when she beat out what she had gleaned, it was about an ephah of barley.\f + \fr 2:17 \ft An ephah is approximately 20 dry quarts or 22 liters (probably about 29 pounds or 13.2 kilograms of barley).\f* 
\v 18 She picked up the grain and went into the town, where \zwd her mother-in-law|Q2596433\zwd* saw what she had gleaned. And she brought out what she had saved from her meal and gave it to \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Then \zwd her mother-in-law|Q2596433\zwd* asked her, “Where did you glean today, and where did you work? Blessed be the man who noticed you.” 
\b
\m So she told \zwd her mother-in-law|Q2596433\zwd* where she had worked. “The name of the man I worked with today is \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd*,” she said. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Then \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* said to \zwd her daughter-in-law|Q1774982\zwd*, “May he be blessed by the LORD, who has not withdrawn His kindness from the living or the dead.” \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* continued, “The man is a close relative. He is one of our kinsman-redeemers.\f + \fr 2:20 \ft The Hebrew word for kinsman-redeemer or guardian-redeemer is a legal term for the kinsman who redeems or vindicates a relative; see Leviticus 25:25–55.\f*” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then \zwd Ruth the Moabitess|Q1774982\zwd* said, “He also told me, ‘Stay with my young men until they have finished gathering all my harvest.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* said to \zwd her daughter-in-law Ruth|Q1774982\zwd*, “\zwd My daughter|Q1774982\zwd*, it is good for you to work with his young women, so that nothing will happen to you in another field.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 So \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd* stayed close to the servant girls of \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* to glean grain until the barley and wheat harvests were finished. And she lived with \zwd her mother-in-law|Q2596433\zwd*. 
\c 3
\s1 Ruth’s Redemption Assured
\m 
\v 1 One day \zwd \zwd Ruth’s|Q1774982\zwd* mother-in-law Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* said to her, “\zwd My daughter|Q1774982\zwd*, should I not seek a resting place for you, that it may be well with you? 
\v 2 Now is not \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd*, with whose servant girls you have been working, a relative of ours? In fact, tonight he is winnowing barley on the threshing floor. 
\v 3 Therefore wash yourself, put on perfume, and wear your best clothes. Go down to the threshing floor, but do not let \zwd the man|Q212253\zwd* know you are there until he has finished eating and drinking. 
\v 4 When he lies down, note the place where he lies. Then go in and uncover his feet, and lie down, and he will explain to you what you should do.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 “I will do everything you say,” \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd* answered. 
\v 6 So she went down to the threshing floor and did everything \zwd her mother-in-law|Q2596433\zwd* had instructed her to do. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 After \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* had finished eating and drinking and was in good spirits, he went to lie down at the end of the heap of grain. Then \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd* went in secretly, uncovered his feet, and lay down. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 At midnight, \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* was startled, turned over, and there lying at his feet was a woman! 
\b
\m 
\v 9 “Who are you?” he asked. 
\b
\m “I am your servant \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd*,” she replied. “Spread the corner of your garment \f + \fr 3:9 \ft Or Spread your wing; the word for wing can also mean corner of a garment; see Ruth 2:12.\f* over me, for you are a kinsman-redeemer.\f + \fr 3:9 \ft The Hebrew word for kinsman-redeemer or guardian-redeemer is a legal term for the kinsman who redeems or vindicates a relative; similarly in verses 12 and 13; see Leviticus 25:25–55.\f*” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* said, “May the LORD bless you, my daughter. You have shown more kindness now than before, because you have not run after the younger men, whether rich or poor. 
\v 11 And now do not be afraid, my daughter. I will do for you whatever you request, since all my fellow townspeople know that you are a woman of noble character. 
\v 12 Yes, it is true that I am a kinsman-redeemer, but there is \zwd a redeemer nearer than I|\zwd*.\f + \fr 3:12 \ft That is, more closely related\f* 
\v 13 Stay here tonight, and in the morning, if he wants to redeem you, good. Let him redeem you. But if he does not want to redeem you, as surely as the LORD lives, I will. Now lie here until morning.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 So she lay down at his feet until morning, but she got up before anyone else could recognize her. 
\b
\m Then \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* said, “Do not let it be known that \zwd a woman|Q1774982\zwd* came to the threshing floor.” 
\v 15 And he told her, “Bring the shawl you are wearing and hold it out.” When she did so, he poured in six measures of barley \f + \fr 3:15 \ft Possibly six seahs (two ephahs), or approximately 39.6 dry quarts or 43.8 liters (about 58 pounds or 26.3 kilograms of barley) \f* and placed it on her. Then he went \f + \fr 3:15 \ft Most Hebrew manuscripts; many Hebrew manuscripts, Vulgate, and Syriac she went\f* into the city. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 When \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd* returned to \zwd her mother-in-law|Q2596433\zwd*, \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* asked her, “How did it go, my daughter?” 
\b
\m Then \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd* told her all that \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* had done for her. 
\v 17 And she said, “He gave me these six measures of barley, for he said, ‘Do not go back to \zwd your mother-in-law|Q2596433\zwd* empty-handed.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 “Wait, \zwd my daughter|Q1774982\zwd*,” said \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd*, “until you find out how things go, for he will not rest unless he has resolved the matter today.” 
\c 4
\s1 Boaz Redeems Ruth
\m 
\v 1 Meanwhile, \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* went to the gate and sat down there. Soon \zwd the kinsman-redeemer|\zwd* \f + \fr 4:1 \ft The Hebrew word for kinsman-redeemer or guardian-redeemer is a legal term for the kinsman who redeems or vindicates a relative; also in verses 3, 6, 8, and 14; see Leviticus 25:25–55.\f* of whom he had spoken came along, and Boaz said, “Come over here, my friend, and sit down.” So he went over and sat down. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 Then \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* took ten of the elders of the city and said, “Sit here,” and they did so. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 And he said to the kinsman-redeemer, “\zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd*, who has returned from the land of Moab, is selling the piece of land that belonged to \zwd our brother Elimelech|id="Q363660" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 4 I thought I should inform you that you may buy it back in the presence of those seated here and in the presence of the elders of my people. If you want to redeem it, do so. But if you \f + \fr 4:4 \ft Many Hebrew manuscripts, LXX, Vulgate, and Syriac; most Hebrew manuscripts he\f* will not redeem it, tell me so I may know, because there is no one but you to redeem it, and I am next after you.” 
\b
\m “I will redeem it,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* said, “On the day you buy the land from \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* and also from \zwd Ruth the Moabitess|Q1774982\zwd*, you must also acquire \zwd the widow of the deceased|Q1774982\zwd* in order to raise up the name of \zwd the deceased|Q9026972\zwd* on his inheritance.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zwd The kinsman-redeemer|\zwd* replied, “I cannot redeem it myself, or I would jeopardize my own inheritance. Take my right of redemption, because I cannot redeem it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Now in former times in Israel, concerning the redemption or exchange of property, to make any matter legally binding a man would remove his sandal and give it to the other party, and this was a confirmation in Israel. 
\v 8 So \zwd the kinsman-redeemer|\zwd* removed his sandal and said to \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd*, “Buy it for yourself.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 At this, \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* said to the elders and all the people, “You are witnesses today that I am buying from \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* all that belonged to \zwd Elimelech|Q363660\zwd*, \zwd Chilion|Q30246326\zwd*, and \zwd Mahlon|Q9026972\zwd*. 
\v 10 Moreover, I have acquired \zwd Ruth the Moabitess|Q1774982\zwd*, \zwd \zwd Mahlon’s|Q9026972\zwd* widow|Q1774982\zwd*, as my wife, to raise up the name of \zwd the deceased|Q9026972\zwd* through his inheritance, so that his name will not disappear from among his brothers or from the gate of his home. You are witnesses today.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 “We are witnesses,” said the elders and all the people at the gate. “May the LORD make \zwd the woman|Q1774982\zwd* entering your home like \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* and \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*, who together built up the house of Israel. May you be prosperous in Ephrathah and famous in Bethlehem. 
\v 12 And may your house become like the house of \zwd Perez|id="Q1814684" t="2"\zwd*, whom \zwd Tamar|id="Q1030008" t="2"\zwd* bore to \zwd Judah|id="Q282220" t="2"\zwd*, because of the offspring the LORD will give you by \zwd this young woman|Q1774982\zwd*.” 
\s1 Boaz Marries Ruth
\b
\m 
\v 13 So \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* took \zwd Ruth|Q1774982\zwd*, and she became \zwd his wife|Q1774982\zwd*. And when he had relations with her, the LORD enabled her to conceive, and she gave birth to \zwd a son|Q1135791\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then the women said to \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd*, “Blessed be the LORD, who has not left you this day without a kinsman-redeemer. May his name become famous in Israel. 
\v 15 He will renew your life and sustain you in your old age. For \zwd your daughter-in-law|Q1774982\zwd*, who loves you and is better to you than seven sons, has given him birth.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 And \zwd Naomi|Q2596433\zwd* took \zwd the child|Q1135791\zwd*, placed him on her lap, and became a nurse to him. 
\v 17 The neighbor women said, “A son has been born to \zwd Naomi|Q1774982\zwd*,” and they named him \zwd Obed|Q1135791\zwd*. He became the father of \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd*, the father of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\s1 The Line of David
\r (Matthew 1:1–17; Luke 3:23–38)
\b
\m 
\v 18 Now these are the generations of \zwd Perez|Q1814684\zwd*: 
\b
\li1 \zwd Perez|Q1814684\zwd* was the father of \zwd Hezron|Q4177234\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 19 \zwd Hezron|Q4177234\zwd* was the father of \zwd Ram|Q1824842\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 \zwd Ram|Q1824842\zwd* was the father of \zwd Amminadab|Q2038223\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 20 \zwd Amminadab|Q2038223\zwd* was the father of \zwd Nahshon|Q1784980\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 \zwd Nahshon|Q1784980\zwd* was the father of \zwd Salmon|Q1771358\zwd*,\f + \fr 4:20 \ft A few Hebrew manuscripts, some LXX manuscripts, and Vulgate (see also verse 21 and LXX of 1 Chronicles 2:11); most Hebrew manuscripts Salma\f* 
\b
\li1 
\v 21 \zwd Salmon|Q1771358\zwd* was the father of \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 \zwd Boaz|Q212253\zwd* was the father of \zwd Obed|Q1135791\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 22 \zwd Obed|Q1135791\zwd* was the father of \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 and \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd* was the father of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
